📣 Pub/Sub vs Event Streaming

Both are event-driven styles.
Difference is mainly: fire-and-forget distribution vs durable, replayable log.


═══════════════════════════════════════
1) Pub/Sub (Publish-Subscribe)
═══════════════════════════════════════

What it is:
  Publishers send messages to a topic/exchange.
  Subscribers receive copies via a broker.
  Publisher and subscriber don’t know each other (decoupled).

Core ideas:
  - Decoupled services
  - One-to-many (one event → many subscribers)
  - Focus: “notify others that something happened”
  - Retention often short (queue/topic drained after consume)
    unless durable subscriptions are configured

Classic mental model:
  Message delivered → consumed → often gone (not a long history to replay)

Tools (examples):
  Google Cloud Pub/Sub
  Amazon SNS (+ SQS for fan-out)
  RabbitMQ (fanout / topic exchanges)
  Kafka can ALSO be used in a pub/sub style (consumer groups)
    but Kafka’s storage model is streaming/log-based

Example:
  UserService publishes UserRegistered
    → EmailService     (welcome mail)
    → AnalyticsService (track signup)
    → CRMService       (create lead)


═══════════════════════════════════════
2) Event Streaming
═══════════════════════════════════════

What it is:
  Continuous stream of events written to an append-only log.
  Events are stored, ordered, and can be re-read (replay).

Core ideas:
  - Durable retention (hours/days/forever by config)
  - Ordered per partition/shard
  - Replayable (reprocess from offset / timestamp)
  - High throughput, many independent consumers
  - Focus: “keep a history of facts and process the stream”

Mental model:
  Events append to a log → consumers track their position (offset)
  New consumer can start from earliest / latest / specific offset

Tools (examples):
  Apache Kafka (most common in interviews)
  Apache Pulsar
  Amazon Kinesis
  Redpanda

Example:
  Click events → Kafka topic
    → FraudDetectionService   (real-time)
    → RecommendationService   (real-time)
    → DataLakeIngestor        (same stream → S3 / analytics)


═══════════════════════════════════════
3) Side-by-side (readable)
═══════════════════════════════════════

Purpose
  Pub/Sub         → distribute messages / trigger work
  Event Streaming → process + store continuous event history

Retention
  Pub/Sub         → usually short-lived (unless durable)
  Event Streaming → long-lived, configurable retention

Replay
  Pub/Sub         → limited / not the main design goal
  Event Streaming → first-class (rewind offsets)

Ordering
  Pub/Sub         → often not guaranteed globally
  Event Streaming → ordered per partition/shard

Consumers
  Pub/Sub         → subscribers (push or pull)
  Event Streaming → consumer groups reading a log at their own pace

Typical use
  Pub/Sub         → emails, notifications, simple microservice triggers
  Event Streaming → analytics, audit, event sourcing, CQRS, clickstreams

Example tools
  Pub/Sub         → SNS, RabbitMQ, Google Pub/Sub
  Event Streaming → Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis


═══════════════════════════════════════
4) Important interview nuance(bareeki) (Kafka)
═══════════════════════════════════════

Kafka is an event streaming platform (commit log).
You can implement pub/sub ON TOP of Kafka:
  - one topic
  - multiple consumer groups
  - each group gets all messages (fan-out)

>>>>>>>>>>Fan-out = one message goes out to MANY consumers (1 → N).
          Like a fan opening: one center point, many blades.

So:
  Pattern “pub/sub”     = how you route to many consumers
  Model “event stream”  = durable ordered log you can replay

Don’t say “Kafka is only pub/sub” or “Kafka can’t do pub/sub”.
Say: Kafka is log-based streaming; pub/sub is a usage pattern on it.


═══════════════════════════════════════
5) When to use which
═══════════════════════════════════════

Use Pub/Sub when:
  - simple notify / trigger other services
  - you don’t need long history or replay
  - classic microservice fan-out (email, push, webhooks)

Use Event Streaming when:
  - real-time processing + analytics on the same data
  - need replay / rebuild state / event sourcing / CQRS
  - high throughput, many independent consumers at different speeds
  - audit trail of what happened


═══════════════════════════════════════
6) What is Fan-out?
═══════════════════════════════════════

Fan-out = one message goes out to MANY consumers (1 → N).
Like a fan opening: one center point, many blades.

Picture:

         UserRegistered event
                  │
                  ▼
               Broker
             /   |   \
            ▼    ▼    ▼
         Email Analytics CRM

One publish → every interested subscriber gets a copy (or sees the same event).


Fan-out vs competing consumers:

  Fan-out (broadcast)
    Goal     → notify MANY different services
    Each msg → Email AND Analytics AND CRM all process it
    Example  → UserRegistered → 3 systems

  Competing / work queue
    Goal     → share work among SAME type of workers
    Each msg → only ONE worker processes it
    Example  → 1000 email jobs → 5 email-worker instances split the load


Where you hear “fan-out”:
  - RabbitMQ fanout exchange → every bound queue gets a copy
  - Amazon SNS fan-out → one topic → many SQS / HTTP / email targets
  - Kafka → one topic + multiple consumer groups = fan-out between groups
    (inside ONE group, partitions are shared = competing, not full fan-out)


One-liner:
  Fan-out = broadcast one event to many independent subscribers,
  each doing its own job.


═══════════════════════════════════════
7) Tiny flow pictures
═══════════════════════════════════════

Pub/Sub:

  Publisher → Broker/Topic → Subscriber A
                           → Subscriber B
                           → Subscriber C

Event Streaming (log):

  Producer → [ e1 e2 e3 e4 e5 ... ] topic/partition log
                ↑         ↑
         Consumer group A reads…   Consumer group B can replay from e1


✍️ Sample Interview Answer
"Pub/Sub is a messaging pattern for decoupled one-to-many delivery—publishers emit events and subscribers react, often with short retention.
Event streaming, like Kafka, stores events in a durable ordered log so consumers can process in real time and replay history.
I use pub/sub for notifications and simple triggers; I use streaming when I need retention, ordering per partition, replay, or event-sourcing style processing.
Kafka can serve pub/sub-style fan-out via consumer groups, but its core model is the event stream."


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Pub/Sub distributes events to subscribers; event streaming stores a durable, ordered, replayable log of events—Kafka is the usual streaming example.”
